      "dom_id": "question-72439",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-xy=x+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-xy & =x+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-xy-x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-xy-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-xy-x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-xy-x)j \\\\  & =(2y-x)\\,i+(y+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2y-x)\\,i+(y+1)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2y-x & =0 \\\\ x & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2y$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-(2y)y-(2y) & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ -{y}^{2}-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=-1$, we get $x=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,-1).$",
